#!/usr/bin/env bash
# deck-ssh - talk to the Steam Deck, but only while holding its queue claim.
#
# "Don't poll the machine under test" cost a run once already: an ssh login
# lands in the `other` CPU column the disturbance verdict reads, and rejected
# a run at 18.4% foreign CPU. This refuses rather than reminds.
set -euo pipefail
DECK="${DECK_HOST:-steamdeck}"
if [ -z "${DECK_QUEUE_TICKET:-}" ]; then
  cat >&2 <<'MSG'
deck-ssh: refusing — you do not hold the Deck.

The Deck runs one benchmark at a time. Take a ticket and run your work inside it:

    deck-queue run <name> -- <your command>

and call that as a *background* Bash command so you are re-invoked when it
finishes. Inside that command DECK_QUEUE_TICKET is set and this wrapper works.

Never ssh the Deck while someone else's run is in flight: the login itself
shows up in the measurement. `deck-queue status` says who holds it.
MSG
  exit 77
fi
exec ssh -o ConnectTimeout=15 -o BatchMode=yes "$DECK" "$@"
